KegAlign `find_hsps` reads `r_chr` and `q_chr` before they are ever assigned.

Both are declared once outside the seed-hit loop and only written inside the
`if (in bounds)` guard, so on each warp's first hit every lane that falls off the
end of a sequence indexes `count[]` / `count_del[]` with an uninitialised `char`.
Those arrays are `short[4]`; any index above 3 writes out of bounds and corrupts
whatever the compiler placed next to them. The corrupted counters feed the
entropy correction, so a few HSPs come out with scores that depend on register
residue — adding an unrelated `__shared__` variable is enough to change them.

This is the only behavioural difference between KegAlign and hspz. hspz
implements the defined behaviour; apply this to make the two agree exactly.

Reproduce: `tests/parity.sh`. Without the patch the `repeat` fixture differs by
one HSP (3764 vs 3747); with it, every fixture is byte-identical.

--- a/src/seed_filter.cu
+++ b/src/seed_filter.cu
@@ -263,8 +263,8 @@
     int temp;
     short count[4];
     short count_del[4];
-    char r_chr;
-    char q_chr;
+    char r_chr = 0;
+    char q_chr = 0;
     uint32_t ref_pos;
     uint32_t query_pos;
     int pos_offset;
